_index":3},{"id":"en-c20-t3-l-p4","title":"Part 4","part_number":4,"passage_text":"","audio_url":"https://ielts-listening-audio.engnovatemedia.com/1715036-cambridge-ielts-20-academic-listening-3-audio-part-4.mp3","question_groups":[{"id":"en-c20-t3-l-g4-0","type":"source_html","ieltsQuestionType":"table_completion","instructions":"","sourceHeading":"Questions 31-40","sourceHtml":"<div class=\"source-question-section-content\"><div class=\"source-section-content-wrapper\"><p><strong>Complete the notes below.</strong></p><p>Write&nbsp;<strong>ONE WORD ONLY</strong>&nbsp;for each answer.</p><p><strong><span>Inclusive Design</span></strong></p><p><strong>Definition</strong></p><p>- Designing products that can be accessed by a diverse range of people without the need for any <span class=\"source-question-item\"><strong class=\"source-question-number\">31</strong><span data-luma-question=\"en-c20-t3-l-q31\" data-luma-kind=\"text\"></span></span></p><p>- Not the same as universal design: that is design for everyone, including catering for people with <span class=\"source-question-item\"><strong class=\"source-question-number\">32</strong><span data-luma-question=\"en-c20-t3-l-q32\" data-luma-kind=\"text\"></span></span> problems.</p><p><strong>Examples of Inclusive Design</strong></p><p>- <span class=\"source-question-item\"><strong class=\"source-question-number\">33</strong><span data-luma-question=\"en-c20-t3-l-q33\" data-luma-kind=\"text\"></span></span> which are adjustable, avoiding back or neck problems</p><p>- <span class=\"source-question-item\"><strong class=\"source-question-number\">34</strong><span data-luma-question=\"en-c20-t3-l-q34\" data-luma-kind=\"text\"></span></span> in public toilets which are easier to use</p><p>To assist the elderly:</p><p>- Designers avoid using <span class=\"source-question-item\"><strong class=\"source-question-number\">35</strong><span data-luma-question=\"en-c20-t3-l-q35\" data-luma-kind=\"text\"></span></span> in interfaces</p><p>- People can make commands using a mouse, keyboard, or their <span class=\"source-question-item\"><strong class=\"source-question-number\">36</strong><span data-luma-question=\"en-c20-t3-l-q36\" data-luma-kind=\"text\"></span></span></p><p><strong>Impact of Non-Inclusive Designs</strong></p><p>Access:</p><p>- Loss of independence for disabled people.</p><p>Safety:</p><p>- Seatbelts are especially problematic for <span class=\"source-question-item\"><strong class=\"source-question-number\">37</strong><span data-luma-question=\"en-c20-t3-l-q37\" data-luma-kind=\"text\"></span></span> women.</p><p>- PPE jackets are often unsuitable because of the size of women’s <span class=\"source-question-item\"><strong class=\"source-question-number\">38</strong><span data-luma-question=\"en-c20-t3-l-q38\" data-luma-kind=\"text\"></span></span></p><p>- PPE for female <span class=\"source-question-item\"><strong class=\"source-question-number\">39</strong><span data-luma-question=\"en-c20-t3-l-q39\" data-luma-kind=\"text\"></span></span> officers dealing with emergencies is the worst.</p><p>Comfort in the Workplace:<strong></strong></p><strong></strong><p><strong></strong>- The <span class=\"source-question-item\"><strong class=\"source-question-number\">40</strong><span data-luma-question=\"en-c20-t3-l-q40\" data-luma-kind=\"text\"></span></span> in offices is often too low for women.</p><div class=\"code-block code-block-14\"></div></div></div><div class=\"source-questions\"><div class=\"source-question-item\"></div><div class=\"source-question-item\"></div><div class=\"source-question-item\"></div><div class=\"source-question-item\"></div><div class=\"source-question-item\"></div><div class=\"source-question-item\"></div><div class=\"source-question-item\"></div><div class=\"source-question-item\"></div><div class=\"source-question-item\"></div><div class=\"source-question-item\"></div></div>","sourceQuestionCount":10}],"audio_fallbacks":["https://engnovate.com/wp-content/upl